1.4 Goals of This Course
This course develops the foundations needed to carry out that workflow. By the end of the course, students should be able to:

translate a substantive problem into an answerable data-science question;
use Python to import, organize, explore, visualize, and model data;
explain the principles behind the methods they use and interpret their results in context;
distinguish descriptive, predictive, and causal claims;
evaluate data quality, model performance, uncertainty, and possible sources of bias;
use Git to maintain a transparent history of a project and collaborate with others;
use Quarto to combine prose, code, results, and citations in a reproducible report;
communicate findings clearly without overstating what the evidence supports; and
identify ethical concerns and take responsibility for analytical choices, including work performed with the assistance of AI.

The aim is not mastery of every available command or algorithm. Tools will change, and unfamiliar problems are unavoidable. The more durable goal is to learn how to select tools, investigate problems, verify results, and explain the reasoning that connects a question to a conclusion.

4.3 Functions
A function should name a task, accept the data and choices needed for that task, and return a result that another part of the analysis can use. It should not quietly depend on a global variable or print a result that a later step needs to analyze. A docstring records the function’s contract, while type hints communicate the intended inputs and output; Python does not automatically enforce those hints.
Two functions can return the same answer while requiring very different amounts of computation. The Fibonacci sequence provides a small example. We use \(F_0 = 0\) and \(F_1 = 1\), so each later value is the sum of the preceding two values. A direct recursive definition is easy to read, but it repeatedly recomputes the same earlier values:

def fibonacci_recursive(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number using direct recursion."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    if n &lt; 2:
        return n
    return fibonacci_recursive(n - 1) + fibonacci_recursive(n - 2)

Memoization stores values that have already been computed. The functools.cache decorator supplies a general-purpose cache, so the recursive definition still mirrors the mathematics without repeating the same work:

from functools import cache


@cache
def fibonacci_memoized(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number while caching earlier results."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    if n &lt; 2:
        return n
    return fibonacci_memoized(n - 1) + fibonacci_memoized(n - 2)

A bottom-up dynamic-programming function builds the sequence from its base cases. It uses linear time and stores all earlier values, which is useful when the analysis needs the sequence rather than only its final value:

def fibonacci_bottom_up(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number using a table of earlier values."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    values = [0, 1]
    for index in range(2, n + 1):
        values.append(values[index - 1] + values[index - 2])
    return values[n] if n else values[0]

When only one value is needed, an iterative function can keep just the two values needed for the next step. It returns the same result with linear time and constant extra memory:

def fibonacci_iterative(n: int) -&gt; int:
    """Return the nth Fibonacci number by iterating from the base cases."""
    if n &lt; 0:
        raise ValueError("n must be nonnegative")
    previous, current = 0, 1
    for _ in range(n):
        previous, current = current, previous + current
    return previous


assert (
    fibonacci_recursive(10)
    == fibonacci_memoized(10)
    == fibonacci_bottom_up(10)
    == fibonacci_iterative(10)
    == 55
)

The recursive version makes the mathematical definition visible, but its repeated work grows exponentially as n increases. Memoization and the two iterative approaches reduce the repeated work to linear time. The bottom-up version uses linear extra memory, while the constant-memory version is the practical choice when only one value is needed. A small timing comparison makes the difference observable:

import timeit

def run_memoized():
    fibonacci_memoized.cache_clear()
    return fibonacci_memoized(30)


timings = {
    "recursive": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_recursive(30), number=1),
    "memoized": timeit.timeit(run_memoized, number=1),
    "bottom_up": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_bottom_up(30), number=1),
    "iterative": timeit.timeit(lambda: fibonacci_iterative(30), number=1),
}
pd.Series(timings, name="seconds")

recursive    0.342256
memoized     0.000036
bottom_up    0.000012
iterative    0.000006
Name: seconds, dtype: float64


Data-science analyses often repeat calculations across observations, simulation trials, features, or model evaluations. An implementation that is acceptable for one small example can become infeasible when the data or number of repetitions grows. Choosing an algorithm, checking its runtime and memory use, and testing equivalent implementations are therefore data-science practices, not merely programming exercises.
A summary function can accept ordinary Python data while returning statistics in a structured form. The function converts its input to a floating-point NumPy array, checks a precondition, and returns a dictionary whose named fields are convenient in prose and tables.

def summarize(values: list[float] | np.ndarray) -&gt; dict[str, int | float]:
    """Return the count, mean, and sample standard deviation of values."""
    data = np.asarray(values, dtype=float)
    if data.ndim != 1 or data.size &lt; 2:
        raise ValueError("values must be a one-dimensional sequence of length 2 or more")
    return {
        "n": int(data.size),
        "mean": float(data.mean()),
        "sd": float(data.std(ddof=1)),
    }

summary = summarize([82, 91, 87, 76, 94])
summary

{'n': 5, 'mean': 86.0, 'sd': 7.176350047203662}


The conversion inside summarize creates an array suited to numerical work. The function therefore accepts an ordinary list but does not force every caller to learn NumPy’s input rules. Its explicit ValueError gives a useful message at the boundary of the function, instead of allowing a mysterious result later in the analysis.
Run a small, direct check whenever you write a function. This is not a complete test suite, but it records a known case and catches an accidental change to the function’s behavior:

expected_mean = 86.0
assert summarize([82, 91, 87, 76, 94])["mean"] == expected_mean

Use a function when a task recurs, when its name clarifies a multi-step calculation, or when you want to test one part of an analysis independently. Do not turn every one-line expression into a function; an unnecessary function can hide a simple calculation.

8.6 Interactive Maps
An interactive map helps when a reader needs to find an area, inspect an exact value, or move between a city view and a street view. It does not fix a poor denominator, uncertain geocode, or unsupported conclusion. The GeoPandas .explore() method uses Folium and Leaflet to map the same ZCTA summaries as the static figures:

Polygon color gives requests per 10,000 estimated residents.
The tooltip gives the ZCTA, request count, population estimate, and rate.
CARTO’s Positron tiles provide a basemap when a key is available; otherwise the example uses OpenStreetMap (CARTO, 2026; GeoPandas Developers, 2026a).

Showing the count and population beside the rate helps a reader understand an unusually high value. Keep the static map as a stable comparison and for readers who cannot use the interactive version. CARTO keys are supplied via the CARTO_BASEMAP_API_KEY environment variable or a local cartoBaseAPIKey.txt file. Because the key appears in browser tile requests, restrict it to the intended site or local preview, keep the file out of Git, and do not publish an HTML page containing an unrestricted key (CARTO, 2026).

import os
from pathlib import Path
from urllib.parse import quote

carto_key = os.environ.get("CARTO_BASEMAP_API_KEY")
if carto_key is None and Path("cartoBaseAPIKey.txt").exists():
    carto_key = Path("cartoBaseAPIKey.txt").read_text().strip()

tile_options = {"tiles": "OpenStreetMap"}
if carto_key:
    tile_options = {
        "tiles": (
            "https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/light_all/"
            f"{{z}}/{{x}}/{{y}}.png?key={quote(carto_key, safe='')}"
        ),
        "attr": "© OpenStreetMap contributors, © CARTO",
    }

interactive_map = area_map.loc[area_map["population"].gt(0)].explore(
    column="requests_per_10k",
    cmap="viridis",
    tooltip=["zcta", "requests", "population", "requests_per_10k"],
    legend=True,
    **tile_options,
)
interactive_map



Make this Notebook Trusted to load map: File -&gt; Trust Notebook


Figure 8.15: Interactive ZCTA map of recorded requests per 10,000 estimated residents. Hover to inspect the count and denominator.




The map uses two levels of information:

Overview: color lets the reader compare rates across areas.
On demand: a tooltip gives the exact numerator and denominator behind a selected rate.

Adding every source field to the tooltip would make inspection harder rather than clearer. The interactive map omits 27 areas with zero estimated population because their rates are undefined; the static map marks them in gray. State this difference when showing the maps together. Online tiles can fail, and hover controls can be difficult with a keyboard or touch device. Keep a static figure, concise text description, and accessible table of key values available as alternatives. Ask the same questions as for any plot: which records appear, what is encoded, and what comparison does it support?
Google Maps can provide an alternative basemap for the same ZCTA choropleth. The Maps JavaScript API data layer accepts GeoJSON polygons and supports feature-based styling and click events. The example below transfers the already prepared area_map to GeoJSON, styles ZCTAs by the same requests-per-resident measure, and opens a detail window when a reader selects a polygon (Google for Developers, 2026a). It uses quantile breaks calculated from the displayed areas, so the legend reports the class boundaries used by this dataset; its colors therefore do not match the continuous Folium scale one for one. The Folium version remains the default and uses OpenStreetMap if a CARTO key is unavailable.
The Google version runs only when an API key is supplied through the GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY environment variable. The key must be enabled for the Maps JavaScript API, restricted to that API and to the site or local server that will display the map, and associated with billing. The generated HTML contains the browser key and should stay local; do not commit it or publish it with the book (Google for Developers, 2026c, 2026b).

import json
import os
from html import escape
from pathlib import Path

google_geojson = json.loads(
    area_map.to_crs("EPSG:4326").to_json(drop_id=True)
)
google_geojson_json = json.dumps(
    google_geojson, separators=(",", ":"), allow_nan=False
).replace("&lt;/", "&lt;\\/")
rate_breaks = np.unique(
    area_map["requests_per_10k"].dropna().quantile([0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8])
).tolist()
colors = ["#ffffcc", "#a1dab4", "#41b6c4", "#2c7fb8", "#253494"]
colors = colors[:len(rate_breaks) + 1]
rate_labels = [
    f"≤ {rate_breaks[0]:.2f}",
    *[
        f"{lower:.2f}–{upper:.2f}"
        for lower, upper in zip(rate_breaks[:-1], rate_breaks[1:])
    ],
    f"&gt; {rate_breaks[-1]:.2f}",
]
api_key = escape(os.environ["GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY"], quote=True)
google_map_html = f"""&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
  &lt;meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Illegal-parking requests by ZCTA&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    #map {{ height: 90vh; width: 100%; }}
    #legend {{ background: white; padding: 10px; margin: 10px;
              font: 14px sans-serif; }}
    .swatch {{ display: inline-block; width: 12px; height: 12px;
               margin-right: 6px; }}
  &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;div id="map" role="application"
       aria-label="Interactive map of NYC ZCTA request rates"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
  &lt;script&gt;
    const zctaData = {google_geojson_json};
    const rateBreaks = {json.dumps(rate_breaks)};
    const colors = {json.dumps(colors)};
    const rateLabels = {json.dumps(rate_labels)};

    async function initMap() {{
      const {{ Map, InfoWindow }} = await google.maps.importLibrary("maps");
      const map = new Map(document.getElementById("map"), {{
        center: {{ lat: 40.7128, lng: -74.0060 }},
        zoom: 10,
        mapTypeId: "roadmap",
      }});
      const infoWindow = new InfoWindow();
      map.data.addGeoJson(zctaData);
      map.data.setStyle((feature) =&gt; {{
        const rate = feature.getProperty("requests_per_10k");
        const classIndex = rate == null
          ? -1
          : rateBreaks.findIndex((upper) =&gt; Number(rate) &lt;= upper);
        const fillColor = classIndex &lt; 0
          ? (rate == null ? "#bdbdbd" : colors[colors.length - 1])
          : colors[classIndex];
        return {{
          fillColor: fillColor,
          fillOpacity: 0.7,
          strokeColor: "#ffffff",
          strokeWeight: 1,
        }};
      }});
      map.data.addListener("click", (event) =&gt; {{
        const feature = event.feature;
        const rate = feature.getProperty("requests_per_10k");
        const rateText = rate == null
          ? "Undefined: zero estimated population"
          : `${{Number(rate).toFixed(2)}} requests per 10,000 residents`;
        const content = document.createElement("div");
        content.textContent =
          `ZCTA ${{feature.getProperty("zcta")}} | ` +
          `${{feature.getProperty("requests")}} requests | ` +
          `${{feature.getProperty("population")}} residents | ${{rateText}}`;
        infoWindow.setContent(content);
        infoWindow.setPosition(event.latLng);
        infoWindow.open({{ map }});
      }});

      const legend = document.createElement("div");
      legend.id = "legend";
      legend.innerHTML = rateLabels.map((label, index) =&gt;
        `&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="swatch" style="background:${{colors[index]}}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;${{label}}&lt;/div&gt;`
      ).join("") +
        '&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="swatch" style="background:#bdbdbd"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;Rate undefined&lt;/div&gt;';
      map.controls[google.maps.ControlPosition.RIGHT_BOTTOM].push(legend);
    }}

    window.initMap = initMap;
  &lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;script async
    src="https://maps.googleapis.com/maps/api/js?key={api_key}&callback=initMap&v=weekly"&gt;
  &lt;/script&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
"""
Path("parking_requests_google_zcta.html").write_text(
    google_map_html, encoding="utf-8"
)
print("Wrote parking_requests_google_zcta.html")

The choice between interactive tools should follow the geographic question and the intended reader action. The GeoPandas–Folium–Leaflet route uses the local ZCTA geometries and runs in the book environment; it supports inspection of the area summaries already constructed. A Google map may be appropriate when the question requires a familiar street basemap or a particular Google service, but the optional example requires key management and produces a separate HTML page. Neither tool decides whether a point should be assigned to an area, which distance measure is substantively relevant, or whether a route is a valid proxy for travel time. For example, straight-line distance from a complaint location to a police precinct is different from network distance and does not include dispatch procedures. A project should therefore define the reader’s task, geographic operation, and units before selecting the interface. It should also account for accessibility, reproducibility, and any information sent to an external map service.
